From faeda9965e414b89be47785e96bffa0138dfb341 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 21:58:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tools/dvbridge: the rows of 4:2:2 chroma made by a compute
 pass with a shared tile (the same bytes)

Pass 2 of the source transport makes the rows of 4:2:2 chroma from the composer's 4:2:0 chroma: a
fragment for each pair of rows, which fetches the 12 or 13 rows of 4:2:0 that its two filters read.
Each row of 4:2:0 is so fetched once for every fragment that reads it: 27 million texel fetches for
a 3840x2160 picture without bars, the most of any pass of the bridge's own after the composer's on
a GPU whose samplers are what limits it (an Intel HD 610 has one).

Where OpenGL ES 3.1 makes it, a compute pass makes the same rows: a workgroup of 32 x 4 invocations
makes 32 columns by 8 rows of pass 2 (two each), from a tile of the 8 + span - 1 rows of 4:2:0 that
those read, fetched into shared memory once (5 KB). That is 2.4 or 2.5 fetches a texel where pass 2
makes 12 or 13; a workgroup that makes none of the texels that the transport reads (the rows of the
bars) fetches nothing, as pass 2 fetches nothing there. Each code is pass 2's, floor(c * chroma_read
+ 0.5) of the same texel (exact: a code times a power of two, plus a half), and the sums are the
same integer sums: the same bytes, every texel of the rows.

The technique is that of the pack of YBLOD's Vulkan engine (dangerouslaser/libreelec-yblod,
yblod-engine-vk 2e7f9bbc85, "the CB1 contract pack from a shared chroma tile", and 972e11ad56 for
its workgroups in the bars that load nothing and its tiles sized for each pass), written again for
the passes of this bridge in OpenGL ES 3.1; nothing of its code is taken.

Fail-closed: without OpenGL ES 3.1, or where the program is not made, pass 2 as before; where the
compute pass raises a GL error, the rows of that frame are made again by pass 2 at once and the
compute pass is not tried again on that GPU. The reference of a check of the bytes on hardware
(video/dvverify) has its own packer, which is never asked for it: a check compares the compute pass
with pass 2. advancedsettings.xml turns it off, to measure what it saves (the GPU time of "DV rows"
with dvpasstimes): <video><dvcomputerows>false</dvcomputerows></video>. kodi.log says once for each
renderer which pass makes the rows.

Measured on Mesa's zink over lavapipe (the software bridge, the libplacebo of the tree): the
transport of the direct and of the composed way with pass 2 and with the compute pass, 28 of 28
files the same byte for byte: three pictures of a profile 7 FEL stream with bars, in the composer's
YCbCr, in IPT, at fidelity step-down level 3, and with a GUI drawn over; a profile 5 chart as
composed and in IPT. A tile read one column off and a group of rows taken for one of the bars, each
a mutation of the compute pass, make every file differ. Not run on a GPU: whether it is faster
there is for the GPU time of the pass to say.
